WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.779 --> 00:00:02.000
- [Emma] Here's a bird's eye view

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.660
of the monumental shifts happening

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.240
across the American workforce right now.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.990
The lines above zero show the sectors

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.550
that saw the strongest job growth,

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.250
since right before the pandemic.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.930
While the lines below show the ones

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.030
that have seen the biggest declines.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.040
It's evidence of a larger phenomenon

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.770
that is touching almost every industry.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.390
- Business is closing their doors,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.340
employees working crazy hours.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.750
It's all because of the
so-called Great Resignation.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:28.610
- [Emma] In August,

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.360
more than 4 million
workers quit their jobs,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.210
a record 20-year high.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:37.600
- A lot of people are
taking time to reevaluate

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.550
what they want to do with their lives

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.090
and what work actually means to them.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.030
- [Emma] For millions of
workers and businesses,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.960
the Great Resignation is a period

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.750
that could define the
future of American labor.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.260
This is how we got to this point

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.033
and what we know about
where the workers are going.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.890
The first thing to know is that this chart

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.494
is showing just a slice of the labor force

00:00:58.494 --> 00:01:01.860
between February, 2020
and September, 2021,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.560
from the Labor Department's
September jobs report.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.400
There are hundreds of other industries

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.860
that have seen growth and declines

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.370
during this period as well.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.300
Some that didn't have data
available for September.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.500
But what the numbers suggest is clear,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.470
thousands of workers lost
their jobs in certain sectors,

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.490
and many aren't returning.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.510
Let's go back to spring 2020
when the pandemic took hold.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.050
- Weekly unemployment claims
had a stunning new high today

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.760
with 6.6 million Americans
filing just last week.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.950
- [Emma] As the pandemic spread,

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.500
businesses shuttered their
doors and tens of millions

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.520
of people lost their jobs.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.780
To help workers and
businesses stay afloat,

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.750
the federal government began
handing out relief payments.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.700
And if we look widely at the labor data,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.640
it's clear that certain
industries lost more workers

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.560
and had a harder time gaining
them back than others.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.020
Employment in motion picture

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.190
and sound recording
industries is still 23% lower

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.290
than it was in February, 2020.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.740
And other sectors hit hardest

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.800
by the pandemic have been
travel and hospitality.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.820
It's clear if you look at this line,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.700
employment and travel arrangement
and reservation services

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.530
is still almost 25% below
February, 2020 levels.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.850
The biggest decline among job
sectors in September data.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.140
- The weaker job growth

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.750
in travel related sectors
is probably a reflection

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.750
of both companies
inability to find workers

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.920
but also weaker demand for
travel related services,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.090
especially as the pandemic
continued during the summer.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.110
- [Emma] Sarah Chaney
Cambon has been reporting

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.620
on the Great Resignation
and American labor shortage

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.070
for the Wall Street Journal.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.540
She pointed out that transit

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.660
and ground passenger transportation jobs

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.620
also saw a huge hit, losing over 18%.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.640
One part of this sector

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.340
that has been impacted
heavily is ride sharing,

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.260
which has experienced a
nationwide shortage of drivers.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.100
And while some have slowly returned

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.780
as government relief has ended,

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.250
many of these workers are
staying off the roads.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.410
For workers with demanding,
lower paying jobs,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.050
the pandemic relief payments

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.590
may have made them less
motivated to return.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.370
And another concern weighing heavily

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.650
on many has been the
risk of getting COVID.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.850
Between mid June and early October,

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.770
the number of people who
said they couldn't work

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.830
because they were sick
with or caring for someone

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.940
with COVID rose by 2 million.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.180
Also during this time,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.350
some older workers chose to
reassess their priorities.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.540
The Federal Reserve
Bank of Dallas estimates

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.070
that 2 1/2 million people
retired during the pandemic,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.540
about twice as many as in 2019.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.490
And that approximately 1.5
million of these workers

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.940
would not have retired if the
pandemic had not happened.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.100
- So a lot of baby
boomers have retired early

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.960
during the pandemic in part
because of concerns about COVID.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.100
Others have seen, really, big
gains from the stock market

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.730
or housing prices going up a ton

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.760
and so they're in a comfortable
financial situation.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:53.870
- [Emma] But many of the workers

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.090
who are leaving jobs aren't
leaving the workforce entirely.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.830
- A lot of people are quitting their jobs,

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.440
maybe because they've experienced burnout

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:04.220
during the pandemic,
or they're just looking

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.810
for different opportunities.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.240
- [Emma] And an increasing number

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.440
of these workers are switching jobs.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.420
- If you're seeing workers
switch from one industry

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.140
to another it's oftentimes
because they can get better pay

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.760
or better working
conditions in the industry

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.370
that they're transitioning to.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.660
- [Emma] Some employers
are offering higher wages

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.290
and healthcare benefits to
try and attract new workers.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.130
And the US economy added
about 3 million jobs

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.300
from April through September this year.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.110
Though, employment was
still down over 3% compared

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.290
with the month before the pandemic hit.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.500
Still, many of these workers
have started jobs in industries

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.250
that have benefited from
pandemic driven shifts.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.597
- E-commerce is really taken off

00:04:43.597 --> 00:04:47.560
and that's created a lot
of jobs at warehouses,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.160
at manufacturers and shipping
and postal companies.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.590
And we see that very
clearly in the job data.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.480
- [Emma] Couriers and messenger jobs saw

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.580
more than an 18% increase,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.280
the largest of any sector
in the September data.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.300
While the warehousing
and storage field saw

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.490
over an 11% rise.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.730
- Some industries were doing really well

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.020
before the pandemic started

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.980
and the pandemic only helps them,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.200
anything related to science and research

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.570
and development was
growing pretty strongly

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.880
before the pandemic started

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.030
and then, after you
had demand for vaccines

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.890
and so that definitely
creates a lot of jobs.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.870
- [Emma] You can see it here.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.740
Scientific research and
development service jobs grew

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:31.470
by almost 10%.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.490
COVID cases have declined since
the September jobs report.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.330
And some economists are optimistic

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.950
that more workers will return this fall.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.500
Still, others are concerned

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.253
that labor shortages reflect
longer-term shifts away

00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:45.680
from the workforce that won't reverse.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.890
Meaning, some of these
industries could be faced

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.793
with long lasting changes.

